hey this is Sarah with registered nurse  Orion calming and today I want to show  you how to administer a so  innama many times in the hospital your  patient will be ordered some type of  enema there's different types are split  Intimus there's KX lait enemas or soap  suds enemas just to name a few  today I'm going to show you how to do  the soap suds enema here we have our bag  this is about a thousand milliliter bag  I filled it up to about the 500  milliliter part and I have filled it  with warm water whenever you're filling  your bag you'll want to check the  temperature with your forearm just to  make sure it's not too hot not too cold  you want it warm and you'll fill it up  to the prescribed amount the doctor  usually writes how much to give and  you'll be putting in side of it it comes  with a packet of soap side and you'll  mix it up and what we'll do is we will  insert this this is the part of the end  of a bag that will go inside the rectum  and you'll slowly release this fluid  into the rectum our goal is to cleanse  the rectum from any part of the stool  that's built up or just to get the  ballast clean a lot of patients are  order this before surgery but in most  cases it's ordered for a patient who's  experiencing um constipation so to do  this she'll get your supplies you'll  make sure soap suds enema and you'll get  your patient always explained to your  patient while you're doing with this  procedure and if you have someone who  can help you of course get help with  this as well so get your patient we have  Miss Smith or mannequin who will be  helping us with this procedure we have  her on her left side and we have her  right leg sort of bent this just helps  the fluid guide into the rectum what  you'll do is you'll take your cap off of  your bag and you lubricate this very  well because it's a hard plastic piece  and  want this to be comfortable as  comfortable as it can be whenever it's  inserted into the patient you'll hang  your bags on your IV pole and get any  towels drapes anything you need because  this procedure can get messy you also  might want to use a gown and the little  blue isolation gallons we have stocked  on the units you'll probably want to  wear one of those to help protect  yourself so what you'll do and check the  area for any hemorrhoids and you're  leading anything that you wouldn't want  to insert something like this so just  look at the area lubricate your tip you  have a clamp here and you'll want to  make sure your clamp is away from the  fluid and watch out we'll leave  sometimes and you have it lubricated and  you're going to start insert into the  rectum about three to four inches okay  always if the patient tells you that  hurts or you meet with distance stop and  you can try again  after you have inserted about three to  four inches you're going to hold your  bag about 12 to 18 inches above the  rectum and slowly let your clamp release  and let the fluid flow in warn your  patient that they may feel some pressure  cramping and if this is so stop your bag  for about 30 seconds and then start it  again a little bit slower until all the  fluid has infused into the rectum takes  a couple minutes but just take your time  and it'll be flowing in and then  whenever it's done after all your fluid  has float floating you'll take it out  and clean the area you may have a little  bit of stool on your end of the cap just  clean the area and throw away your bag  and tell the paint and cover up your  patient and tell your patient to hold  the fluid in for about five to ten  minutes and this just helps break up any  hard stool and helps get the most  effectiveness out of the enema this is  hard for a lot of patients because they  describe feeling a lot of pressure in  their bottom and so if they can't hold  it just you know get them to the  bathroom or get them on the bedpan so  after that and when every patient has a  bowel movement be sure to look at that  see how effective the enema was and see  if it has to be repeated so that is  exactly how you do a success enema and  if you liked this video or need any  other nursing skill videos please check  out our website register nurse or in  comm and thank you so much for watching
 

